The backbone uses 3D rotary position embeddings (3D-RoPE) to encode temporal and spatial token coordinates before self-attention. For latent features x∈ℝB×S×Cx\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times S\times C} with S=F​H​WS=FHW, the channel dimension is partitioned across temporal, height, and width axes, and RoPE is applied separately to the corresponding coordinate subspaces before concatenation. In Wan, each RoPE dimension has a fixed maximum sequence length of 1024; although RoPE remains mathematically defined beyond this range, generation outside the positional regime observed during training can degrade attention quality.

